---
/**
 * The title bar on an inner page: the same right-attached column the homepage
 * opens with, but compact, and pinned to the top so it stays readable while the
 * page's content rolls up underneath it.
 *
 * It carries no photograph of its own. On these pages the picture is fixed to
 * the viewport by the layout's `backdrop` slot and stays still while the
 * content scrolls over it, so a second copy inside this bar would scroll away
 * and tear away from the one behind it.
 *
 * A full-screen hero was the option rejected at mockup, and even the 448px band
 * that replaced it gave the reader a third of a screen of scenery before the
 * first rule. Someone arriving at an inner page has come to read something,
 * usually after scanning a printed card outdoors; every pixel this bar takes is
 * a pixel of what they came for.
 *
 * `sticky` rather than `fixed`: it holds its place in the flow, so the content
 * below starts under it rather than beneath an overlay, and it pins on its own
 * once it reaches the top. Nothing here needs to know the header's height.
 */
interface Props {
  title: string;
  lead?: string;
}

const { title, lead } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="sticky top-24">
  {/*
    Frosted rather than transparent. A see-through bar would let the cards
    travel visibly *through* the title as they scroll under it; blurring what
    passes behind gives them somewhere to go. The wash is sand, so the
    photograph still reads through the bar rather than being boxed out of it.

    It reaches 7rem above its own box, which is more than the navigation's
    height, and pins at `top-24`, which is a little less. Those two numbers are
    deliberately not equal and neither has to be exact: the overshoot covers
    everything from above the viewport down to the title, so the nav sits in the
    same unbroken band and no sliver of scrolling card can appear between them.
    Backed only to its own top edge, the frosting started below the nav and read
    as a stray blurred stripe laid across the photograph.

    No `z-index` on the section, deliberately: the header carries `z-10`, so
    leaving this at the default keeps the overshoot *behind* the nav rather
    than frosting over it, while still painting above the cards, which are not
    positioned at all.
  */}
  <div class="frost-on-scroll mask-fade-bottom absolute inset-x-0 -top-28 bottom-0 bg-sand/75 backdrop-blur-sm"></div>

  <div class="relative mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl px-4 py-5">
    <div class="max-w-xl">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-brand sm:text-4xl">{title}</h1>
      {/* The shorter measure keeps the line endings off the busiest part of the
          photograph, the same reason the homepage's lead carries it. */}
      {lead && <p class="mt-2 max-w-md">{lead}</p>}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
